{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'PAID-GATE|paid|guard|preflight|readiness|pre-flight' AGENTS.md | tail -58; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_rehearsal.py | head -34; rg -n 'select|provider|_key|network' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/round47h-brief.md docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md | head -24; ls -al /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/*/scratchpad | rg -i 'send|launch|gate-d753|auth|r70|v13s' | tail -25; rg -n 'computed outcome|85%|shortfall' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind7-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json | tail -3\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'PAID-GATE|paid|guard|preflight|readiness|pre-flight' AGENTS.md | tail -58; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_rehearsal.py | head -34; rg -n 'select|provider|_key|network' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/round47h-brief.md docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md | head -24; ls -al /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/*/scratchpad | rg -i 'send|launch|gate-d753|auth|r70|v13s' | tail -25; rg -n 'computed outcome|85%|shortfall' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind7-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json | tail -3\"","aggregated_output":"47:  provider caps are untouched; paid spending stays within D439's ceiling. A disagreement the council cannot settle is recorded with\n49:- Add no mechanism, safeguard or substitute rule for a case no one has named. \"No special handling\" keeps the pipeline's shape as it is.\n153:- Tests never reach a paid service or load a credential: `tests/conftest.py` replaces the model and Jev key readers with a refusal, so a\n182:- Before any paid run, `python3 scripts/preflight.py` replays the stored model answers and matching records of the archives and ledgers\n186:  diagnosed before the run; a paid run is never the way a check or a prompt is first tried.\n234:  identity before a paid send.\n357:  analysis stability test. This is a principle you should remember\"). A day or week slice before any whole-corpus run; preflight on\n358:  stored data before any paid run (§5); no edit while a run is live (§9). A paid run measures a configuration; it never tries a fix.\n371:  process error (what grew, what the small sample did not guard) and the mechanism, traced to the raw answers and the model's account,\n372:  never a guess stated as the cause. A small sample guards every class the change can move, not only its own. When a fix keeps moving\n421:- Inspect a path before writing to it; new stored data gets a new, dated name; chain guarded steps with `&&`. Deleting stored data needs\n427:  scored against the gold). A full-pipeline run, a registered test, or a paid send beyond the probes of a few cents an agreed direction\n429:- A long paid run is launched detached in a new session with a log and pid file, after checking machine and network health; nothing heavy\n430:  runs beside paid calls. A gate runs on a snapshot no one is editing.\n     1\t\"\"\"$0 rehearsal of the judging-v13 paid gate: the provider stubbed at urllib.request.urlopen beneath the real budget transport, the real\n     2\tmodel.openai_transport and the real stop gate. Every HTTP body must equal, byte for byte, a request built here independently from the\n     3\tplan. Usage: python3 paid_rehearsal.py <scratch folder>   (runs every scenario; prints one line per check; exit 1 on any failure).\"\"\"\n     4\timport io\n     5\timport json\n     6\timport os\n     7\timport shutil\n     8\timport sys\n     9\timport urllib.error\n    10\timport urllib.request\n    11\tfrom pathlib import Path\n    12\t\n    13\tHERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n    14\tsys.path.insert(0, str(HERE))\n    15\timport paid_gate as pg                                                  # noqa: E402\n    16\tfrom hivemind import model                                              # noqa: E402\n    17\t\n    18\tPLAN = json.loads((HERE / 'paid-plan.json').read_text())\n    19\tBY_USER = {it['user']: it for it in PLAN}\n    20\t\n    21\t\n    22\t\n    23\tclass FakeResponse:\n    24\t    def __init__(self, body):\n    25\t        self.body, self.status = body, 200\n    26\t\n    27\t    def read(self):\n    28\t        return self.body\n    29\t\n    30\t    def __enter__(self):\n    31\t        return self\n    32\t\n    33\t    def __exit__(self, *exc):\n    34\t        return False\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:66:  - a `started` line is written before the provider is called;\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:69:  - a refused reservation never reaches the provider.\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:226:  - The record keeps the provider's whole response, as the archive stores it, with no 20,000-character cut.\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:246:    provider's own call sequence.\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/round47h-brief.md:3:The 47g finding is answered (PAID-GATE.md §3h): scored first attempts are journalled in answers.jsonl and diagnostic retries in retries.jsonl (`Gate.record`); `attempt_records` reads both, splitting on '\\n' only (the writer's delimiter), and labels each damaged line with its journal; `score` counts only answers.jsonl damage toward INCOMPLETE; `run_digest` covers both journals. Rehearsed: a torn retry record → reported, retry interrupted, verdict complete; the torn scored record control → INCOMPLETE; an answer holding U+2028, U+0085 and U+2029 stays one record; first-before-retry order checked on the provider's own call sequence.\ndrwxr-xr-x@  14 lchingen  wheel       448 Nov 20 17:29 dep-probe-send\ndrwxr-xr-x@   2 lchingen  wheel        64 Nov 21 00:00 july-send-scratch\ndrwxr-xr-x@   3 lchingen  wheel        96 Nov 16 07:17 launcher-mutant\ndrwxr-xr-x@   5 lchingen  wheel       160 Nov 16 07:16 launcher-proof-1\ndrwxr-xr-x@   5 lchingen  wheel       160 Nov 16 07:17 launcher-proof-2\ndrwxr-xr-x@   5 lchingen  wheel       160 Nov 16 07:18 launcher-proof-3\ndrwxr-xr-x@   6 lchingen  wheel       192 Nov 23 02:02 send-guard\n188:      \"basis_text\": \"My forecast of FY2028 Vera CPU revenue growth. NVIDIA guides to more than double (F2, a floor of 100% growth), but this is a full-year target for the next fiscal year for a product whose deployments are still ahead, and no order, backlog, unit or price figure puts FY2028 volume in hand, so the target is discounted rather than taken in full. The no-share-gain benchmark is the server CPU market's roughly 50% annual growth (F4; Bank of America's fivefold path, F7, is consistent), which gives $30 billion (F8). I place the pace at 85%, about 70% of the way from market pace to the guidance floor: management sees its own platform attach, reaffirmed the FY2027 base mid-year and claims adoption by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and OEM plus standalone interest from Anthropic; the remaining gap reflects execution risk on an unproven ramp, an FY2027 base already above half of the third-party CPU market (F9), AMD's competitive claims in standalone sockets, and Vera being only part of the total CPU revenue the original statement covers.\",\n219:      \"reasons\": \"Reference (rung 2): NVIDIA's own guidance. CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 (F1) and expects FY2028 to more than double it (F2) (e3660); e4691 and e4856 report the same claim as Vera CPU revenue, so the $20 billion is the Vera base. $20 billion x 2 = $40 billion (F3), and 'more than' double places the reference just inside rung 2 ($40-50 billion). No consensus figure is supplied. Other evidence has arrived, so the guidance is weighed rather than kept. It is a full-year target for the next fiscal year, not near-term guidance, for a product whose deployments are still ahead: Kress says Vera 'will be deployed' by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and system OEM (e4689), and Anthropic is only evaluating it (e5044). No order, backlog, unit or price figure puts FY2028 volume in hand, and the FY2027 base is itself not yet reported (e4856 calls it a potential), so the target is discounted rather than taken in full. Benchmarks for the pace: AMD sees the server CPU market growing about 50% a year through 2030 (F4, e4687), and Bank of America's $35 billion to $170 billion path (F5, F6; about 4.9x over four years, F7, e4857) is consistent. Growing at market pace, FY2028 Vera revenue would be $30 billion (F8); the guidance floor is $40 billion. A doubling therefore requires share gains on top of market growth, from an FY2027 base that already equals more than half of Bank of America's 2026 CPU market (F9), if the two definitions match. Evidence pushing up: the universal customer adoption claim (e4689), standalone interest for agentic workloads (e5044), Arm-based servers already holding a large share of data-center revenue (e4692), and our own themes that hyperscaler data-center capex runs in the upper-middle range in 2026 and 2027 (v6, v7; v7 updated since the last judgement), which supports demand for the NVIDIA systems that carry Vera but does not by itself supply the share gain a doubling needs. Evidence pushing down: AMD's own benchmarks claim its EPYC and Venice CPUs beat Vera per core, at platform level, in rack throughput, in agentic-AI tests and in memory bandwidth (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5403, e5404); this is a competitor's marketing and weighs lightly, but it bears on standalone sockets, the part of the growth beyond attach to NVIDIA's own systems. The original statement also covers all NVIDIA CPU revenue, of which Vera is a part. e4691, e4764 and e4856 repeat e3660 and move nothing. On balance the evidence favours a shortfall against the doubling but growth well above market pace: I forecast 85% growth (F10), closer to the guidance than to market pace because management sees its own platform attach and reaffirmed FY2027 mid-year, giving $37 billion (F11), rung 3 ($30-40 billion). Reaching rung 2 needs the full doubling, which needs volume evidence not yet shown; rung 4 needs growth at or below market pace despite the adoption claims; both are less likely than a mid-band outcome. A reported FY2027 Vera run-rate at or above the $20 billion pace, or sized FY2028 customer orders, would move this to rung 2.\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && python3 -B -c 'import json,paid_gate as g,re\nroot=g.REPO/\\\"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind7-2026-11-23\\\"; pp=json.loads((g.HERE/\\\"paid-plan-v13s.json\\\").read_text())\nfor case in (\\\"E\\\",\\\"S\\\",\\\"P-v8\\\"):\n d=root/case; it=json.loads((d/\\\"item.json\\\").read_text())\n for p in sorted((d/\\\"answers\\\").glob(\\\"*.json\\\")):\n  x=json.loads(p.read_text()); a=x[\\\"answers\\\"][0]; c=g.decode(x,\\\"v13s\\\",it[\\\"family\\\"]); orig={f[\\\"id\\\"]:f for f in c[\\\"figures\\\"]}; norm,rec,es=g.card_view(c,a[\\\"view_id\\\"],[],ck=g.cs); shapes=lambda f:(f[\\\"what\\\"],f.get(\\\"value\\\"),f.get(\\\"low\\\"),f.get(\\\"kind\\\")); nf={f[\\\"id\\\"]:f for f in norm[\\\"figures\\\"]}; mentioned=set(re.findall(r\\\"\\\\bF\\\\d+\\\\b\\\",json.dumps({k:a[k] for k in (\\\"reasons\\\",\\\"why\\\")}))); me=set(re.findall(r\\\"\\\\be\\\\d+\\\\b\\\",json.dumps(a))) & set(it[\\\"shown\\\"]); print(case,p.stem,\\\"missing figure refs\\\",sorted(mentioned-set(nf)),\\\"wrong mapped f-refs\\\",sorted(k for k in mentioned & nf.keys() & orig.keys() if shapes(nf[k])\"'!=shapes(orig[k])),\"passages missing total\",len(me-set(es)))\n  if case==\"E\" and p.stem==\"codex\":\n   ii=pp[-1]; lines,ids=g.card_lines(ii,ii[\"family\"],a[\"view_id\"],\"K1\",x,\"no\"); print(\"paid card names absent\",sorted(me-ids),\"raw fig ids shown\",sorted(y for y in ids if y.startswith(\"F\")))\n'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
